|> Exercise
    handle=_tan_cot_on_unit_circle_

    Stipulating

    $$
    \,\tan\theta = {\sin\theta\over\cos\theta},
    $$
    $$
    \cot\theta = {\cos\theta\over\sin\theta}
    $$

    as definitions of 
    tan _&_ cot,
    where can we find $\tan(\theta)$
    _&_ $\cot(\theta)$ as geometric quantities
    that exist in relation to the unit circle,
    in all quadrants?
    (I.e., find “unit circle interpretations”
    of 
    tan _&_ cot.)

    |> Solution

        Note that

        $$
        {\sin\theta\over\cos\theta}
        $$

        can be understood as 
        a fraction of the form “rise over run”,
        i.e., the slope of the line from $(0, 0)$ to $(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_tan_cot_unit_circle_1.svg

        However, one way to “materialize” a slope 
        as a displacement
        is to scale the triangle “rise over run” until
        the “run” becomes $1$, 
        whereby the rise becomes equal to the slope,
        leading to this
        construction of $\tan\theta$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_tan_cot_unit_circle_2.svg
            style=margin-bottom:-0.5em

        This also works if $\theta$ is in other
        quadrants, with a negative scaling factor if
        necessary:
        
        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_tan_cot_unit_circle_3.svg
            style=margin-bottom:-0.5em

        Symmetrically, the ratio

        $$
        \cot\theta = {\cos\theta \over \sin\theta}
        $$

        has the form “run over rise”, and can
        be evaluated by scaling the same triangle
        until “rise” becomes $1$, in which case the
        corresponding (scaled) “run” becomes the value
        of $\cot\theta$:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/e5_tan_cot_unit_circle_4.svg

        Overall,
        to give self-contained verbalizations of these
        constructions, we have...

        __$\tan\theta$ is the $y$-coordinate of the
        intersection of the line through the origin
        and $(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$ with the
        line $x = 1$__

        !! ...for tangent, and...
        ...for $\tan\theta$, and...

        __$\cot\theta$ is the $x$-coordinate of the
        intersection of the line through the origin
        and $(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)$ with the
        line $y = 1$__

        ...for $\cot\theta$!

        